1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,800
 Hey everyone, Ryan here, and welcome back to our orthodontics series.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:14,320
 So we've been talking about biomechanics, which is a fancy combination of biology and

3
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:15,320
 mechanics.

4
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:18,380
 The last video we talked about the biology of tooth movement.

5
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:25,600
 In this video, we will talk about the mechanical principles of tooth movement.

6
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:29,940
 So first we have to talk about the center of resistance.

7
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:36,680
 So the center of resistance is a fixed point that a force must pass through in order to

8
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:40,680
 move an object in a straight line.

9
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:43,200
 So let me show you how that works.

10
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:47,400
 Let's say we have a free floating object in space.

11
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:53,160
 The center of resistance would be coincident with the center of mass.

12
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:58,400
 So for this uniform square, the center of resistance is right at its center.

13
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:05,880
 So if we push the square from the left and the force is applied through that center of

14
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:11,800
 resistance, the square will translate to the right.

15
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:19,600
 However, if we were to push on that square, let's say down here for instance, now the

16
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:26,780
 square will still move to the right, but it will also spin around as it does so because

17
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:31,320
 that force isn't being applied through the center of resistance.

18
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:35,800
 You can try this out for yourself with a pencil if you have one handy.

19
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:44,840
 You can put the pencil down on a flat surface and try to push it through its center of resistance.

20
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:51,360
 Now if you find the right spot to push on, the pencil will move straight across the table

21
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:55,720
 without rotating one way or the other.

22
00:01:55,720 --> 00:02:03,120
 But if you press the pencil at either end, you'll see how it starts to spin and that

23
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:08,240
 is the center of resistance at work.

24
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:12,840
 Now for a tooth, it's a little bit different because the tooth isn't floating in space

25
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:15,920
 or laying flat on a table.

26
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:21,040
 It's tethered to bone and soft tissue at its root.

27
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:27,400
 So the center of resistance for a healthy tooth is generally about halfway between the

28
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:32,560
 alveolar crest and the root apex.

29
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:37,160
 So it would put it around the center of the root.

30
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:45,080
 Now the center of resistance tends to be located more apically for a periodontally compromised

31
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:51,680
 tooth that has loss of bone at the alveolar crest and the center of resistance tends to

32
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:59,200
 be located more occlusily for teeth when apical root resorption has happened because there's

33
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:05,960
 less root surface at the bottom and the new center of the clinical crown is further up.

34
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:12,000
 So really in all three of these cases, the center of resistance is located at the center

35
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,120
 of the portion of root that is bound to bone.

36
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:22,840
 Now the center of rotation is different.

37
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:29,760
 So the center of rotation is an unfixed point around which an object is rotating if and

38
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:32,160
 when it is rotating.

39
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:39,720
 Another way to say it is the point about which a body appears to have rotated as determined

40
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:43,600
 from its initial and final position.

41
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:47,280
 So let's go back to the pencil example.

42
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:54,800
 If you push the pencil, let's say down here near the tip or up here near the eraser, it

43
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:59,240
 will tend to rotate as we talked about before.

44
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:07,840
 So let's say we are going to push the pencil near its eraser and let's say the pencil will

45
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:15,040
 spin so that the tip stays relatively still and the rest of the pencil kind of spins around

46
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:17,800
 like this.

47
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:24,680
 So as it's rotating, the part of the pencil that stays where it is and doesn't appear

48
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:30,120
 to move is the center of rotation.

49
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:36,440
 So you can imagine that this center of rotation will change dramatically depending on where

50
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:37,920
 you apply the force.

51
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:48,320
 It is an entirely unfixed point and it's entirely dependent on where that force is being applied.

52
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:54,160
 So it's easy to apply force through the center of resistance for a pencil because you have

53
00:04:54,160 --> 00:05:01,520
 access to its entire length along the pencil if it's lying down on a desk, for instance.

54
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:06,680
 But for a tooth, however, that's a completely different story because we know that the center

55
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:16,920
 of resistance is buried down here and a force that's applied up here at the bracket will

56
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:24,000
 not act through the center of resistance and will cause some kind of rotation of the

57
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,720
 tooth.

58
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:30,800
 So you can imagine that we will be dealing with the center of rotation quite a bit because

59
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:40,040
 it's very difficult to apply force through that center of resistance.

60
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:44,760
 Now of course we've been talking about forces already but I just want to briefly revisit

61
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:46,320
 the basics here.

62
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:54,560
 So a force is a linear vector with both magnitude, some amount of strength and direction.

63
00:05:54,560 --> 00:06:00,280
 It's often represented by a straight arrow in force diagrams.

64
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:06,280
 So we usually would draw the arrow pointing towards an object for a push and draw the

65
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:11,480
 arrow away from an object to describe a pull.

66
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:18,920
 And orthodontics is really just a series of push and pull force systems applied to the

67
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:20,880
 teeth.

68
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:25,200
 Now the point of force application is also crucial.

69
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:31,360
 So not only does a force have magnitude and direction but it's also being applied to some

70
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:35,640
 specific location on the tooth, again usually at the bracket.

71
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:41,760
 Like we talked about before, any force against an object unless the force is directed through

72
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:51,360
 that object center of resistance causes a tendency to rotate which is also called a moment.

73
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:59,560
 So a moment or a moment of a force is the tendency of a force to cause a body to rotate

74
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:04,760
 about a specific axis which is the center of rotation.

75
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:13,000
 So a moment occurs whenever a force is delivered at some distance from the center of resistance.

76
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:20,000
 So the equation to calculate that is moment is equal to force times distance.

77
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:28,240
 So if a force is applied let's say here, again up here by the eraser you would multiply that

78
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:40,480
 forces magnitude by the distance of that application of the force to the center of resistance.

79
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:47,680
 So that's what the distance D is referring to.

80
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:52,720
 So you might notice we're really getting a little bit heavy into the mathematics side

81
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:59,800
 of things but this is almost identical to the equation for torque used in physics.

82
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:05,080
 And the equation is helpful because it helps us understand that the rotational tendency

83
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:12,000
 is stronger the further away the force is applied from the center of resistance.

84
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:16,840
 The bigger this number is the bigger this number will be.

85
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:23,840
 So this is like closing a door so you can take any door that's near you and try one

86
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:30,640
 time to push on that door with your hand close to the hinges of course be careful not to

87
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:37,160
 pinch your fingers but then try again to close that same door this time with your hand on

88
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:42,400
 the handle and notice how much easier it is the second time when you're applying that

89
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:51,600
 force further away from the hinge axis and that's because you have more rotational influence.

90
00:08:51,600 --> 00:09:00,040
 So again just to revisit this concept if we're straight through the center of resistance

91
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:05,480
 that forces being applied straight through there we have no moment because the distance

92
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:07,560
 would be equal to zero.

93
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:15,880
 As soon as we go outside of that there's either going to be a counterclockwise moment

94
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:25,160
 or a clockwise moment that's wrapping around that center of resistance and that's representing

95
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:27,600
 a moment.

96
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:29,720
 Alright so let's keep it going.

97
00:09:29,720 --> 00:09:39,240
 A couple refers to two parallel forces that are equal in magnitude opposite in direction

98
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:46,680
 and non-colinear which means they do not share a line of action in other words they're separated

99
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:55,960
 by some distance so a force couple creates pure rotation that is a pure moment either

100
00:09:55,960 --> 00:10:03,600
 clockwise or counterclockwise which can be incredibly useful in orthodontics as you will

101
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:05,840
 soon see.

102
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:14,440
 So the magnitude of the moment of a couple or MC is equal to the magnitude of one of

103
00:10:14,440 --> 00:10:22,520
 the applied forces times the perpendicular distance between those two forces.

104
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:30,800
 So here is our force couple, one force pointing this way and an equal and opposite force pointing

105
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:38,800
 this way and they are non-colinear so they're acting at some distance from each other.

106
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:46,040
 So in order to have a couple you need to have two points of contact, two separate points

107
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:54,640
 of contact and a wire, an orthodonic wire being engaged into a bracket slot often results

108
00:10:54,640 --> 00:11:04,840
 in two points of contact which makes that very useful to us as orthodontists.

109
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:11,320
 And this is why, so we can look at some examples of couples and how they help us to move teeth

110
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:14,800
 in the desired directions.

111
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:22,800
 So in the first order that's from an occlusal view based on how the wire is pushing against

112
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:31,480
 the base of the bracket slot is applying a couple for us to rotate this tooth.

113
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:41,040
 So the wire is trying to get back to its original shape and so it's pushing on the bracket

114
00:11:41,040 --> 00:11:49,120
 with the bracket base I should say at that point and it's pulling against the ligature

115
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:53,880
 that's tying that wire to that bracket on this side of the bracket.

116
00:11:53,880 --> 00:12:00,640
 So we're having two forces that are equal and opposite and non-colinear.

117
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:10,320
 So this is creating a couple to provide mesiodistal rotation and so it wants to turn that tooth

118
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:15,360
 around in order to straighten out the wire.

119
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:19,040
 So that's an example of a first order couple.

120
00:12:19,040 --> 00:12:26,000
 The second order is from a facial view and it's based on how the wire is pushing down

121
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:35,600
 on one end of the bracket and up on the other end creating a couple this time for mesiodistal

122
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:39,160
 angulation.

123
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:45,640
 And lastly for the third order that's from a proximal or side view and that's based on

124
00:12:45,640 --> 00:12:54,280
 how a rectangular wire is engaged and twisted into the bracket slot so that it pushes on

125
00:12:54,280 --> 00:13:02,920
 one end and down on the other end creating a tiny couple but it can be effective in order

126
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:14,800
 to tip this tooth or I should say torque this tooth for improved buccolingual inclination.

127
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:22,040
 So these are all examples of couples and how orthodontic wires act on brackets to generate

128
00:13:22,040 --> 00:13:23,640
 these four systems.

129
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:28,400
 Now don't worry if you don't understand all the details of this I just wanted to illustrate

130
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:35,000
 some cool examples of these complicated mechanics to give them some clinical application because

131
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,560
 I know this stuff can get pretty tough to understand.

132
00:13:39,560 --> 00:13:44,600
 So now we can apply all of these mechanical principles and terms to the types of tooth

133
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:47,360
 movement that we talked about in the last video.

134
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:51,680
 So the first of those is uncontrolled tipping.

135
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:59,140
 So the moment of couple to moment of force ratio is something that we can now use to

136
00:13:59,140 --> 00:14:02,800
 describe each of these biomechanical movements.

137
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:07,960
 For this one the ratio is equal to zero.

138
00:14:07,960 --> 00:14:16,960
 In other words the moment of the couple is equal to zero which means there's no couple

139
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:23,360
 here which makes sense because couples can be hard to obtain and this is the lowest

140
00:14:23,360 --> 00:14:27,200
 effort and easiest tooth movement that we have.

141
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:33,000
 So if we're using a finger spring that's pushing against a tooth from only one point

142
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,520
 of contact well we're not going to have any couple there and it's just going to produce

143
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:40,320
 uncontrolled tipping.

144
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:46,400
 So in these diagrams that I'll have in the next couple slides the center of resistance

145
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:51,320
 is shown by the red x inside the circle.

146
00:14:51,320 --> 00:14:55,880
 The center of rotation is shown by this black x.

147
00:14:55,880 --> 00:15:02,960
 The force is shown by the straight blue arrow and any moment that's generated is represented

148
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:06,440
 by the curved blue arrows.

149
00:15:06,440 --> 00:15:13,040
 So the center of rotation and center of resistance are almost at the same point.

150
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:19,600
 So the tooth rotates around a point very close to its center of resistance but usually the

151
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:26,080
 center of rotation is just slightly apical to that center of the root.

152
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:34,480
 Now because the force is applied at some distance from that center of rotation we have some

153
00:15:34,480 --> 00:15:41,880
 amount of D so there's going to be a moment that's created to the tooth wants to rotate.

154
00:15:41,880 --> 00:15:48,660
 The crown will go in the direction of the force and the root will go in the opposite

155
00:15:48,660 --> 00:15:50,440
 direction.

156
00:15:50,440 --> 00:15:58,760
 The tooth tips uncontrollably because there is no couple to counteract this moment of

157
00:15:58,760 --> 00:16:00,520
 a force.

158
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:05,000
 So there's no controlling counteracting couple at work.

159
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:11,320
 The uncontrolled tipping is the result of one point of contact on the tooth crown like again

160
00:16:11,320 --> 00:16:15,340
 a finger spring from a removable appliance.

161
00:16:15,340 --> 00:16:23,040
 This is the easiest and fastest type of tooth movement but often undesirable.

162
00:16:23,040 --> 00:16:28,720
 For control tipping the ratio is between 0 and 1.

163
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:37,320
 In other words the moment of a couple is less than the moment of the force.

164
00:16:37,320 --> 00:16:44,040
 So the center of rotation here is moved apically away from the center of resistance and is

165
00:16:44,040 --> 00:16:47,160
 at around the root apex.

166
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:53,280
 So at least this time the crown moves in the direction you want while the root stays more

167
00:16:53,280 --> 00:16:59,680
 or less where it is instead of going back in the opposite direction.

168
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:08,800
 So force applied to a bracket causes translation of the tooth and some amount of rotation while

169
00:17:08,800 --> 00:17:15,180
 the small couple applied to the tooth counteracts and negates that rotation.

170
00:17:15,180 --> 00:17:20,600
 So instead of this root being moved this way like an uncontrolled tipping it stays where

171
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:25,360
 it is thanks to the couple that's being applied.

172
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:32,160
 In this example that would have to be a third order couple provided by a rectangular wire

173
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:37,440
 in an edge wise bracket.

174
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:41,400
 So for bodily movement the ratio is now 1.

175
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:48,040
 It's equal to 1 which means the moment of a couple equals the moment of a force.

176
00:17:48,040 --> 00:17:52,520
 So notice how this moment of a couple is getting stronger and stronger as we go through these

177
00:17:52,520 --> 00:17:54,840
 tooth movements.

178
00:17:54,840 --> 00:18:02,320
 So here the tooth moves bodily or translates and the center of rotation is displaced infinitely

179
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:07,760
 far away from the center of resistance because there is no rotation.

180
00:18:07,760 --> 00:18:16,120
 Ultimately the crown and the root move together the same amount in the same direction.

181
00:18:16,120 --> 00:18:21,880
 In order to get successful bodily movement it requires both a force to move the crown

182
00:18:21,880 --> 00:18:28,200
 and a fairly strong couple to prevent tipping altogether which is why this is one of the

183
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:34,760
 more difficult and slower movements to achieve.

184
00:18:34,760 --> 00:18:39,320
 And then for root torque the ratio now is greater than 1.

185
00:18:39,320 --> 00:18:45,880
 In other words the moment of a couple is greater than the moment of a force.

186
00:18:45,880 --> 00:18:50,880
 So this is a unique type of tooth movement because this is the only time that the root

187
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:54,320
 is moving more than the crown itself.

188
00:18:54,320 --> 00:18:59,840
 Therefore the center of rotation is displaced in the opposite direction and moves towards

189
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:05,640
 the incisal edge which this time stays relatively still.

190
00:19:05,640 --> 00:19:12,360
 This movement is incredibly difficult to achieve and often requires some auxiliary appliances

191
00:19:12,360 --> 00:19:18,960
 because you're trying to move a root through bone while keeping a crown steady which is

192
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:26,240
 sitting in essentially air which is a lot easier to move something through air than it

193
00:19:26,240 --> 00:19:28,540
 is through bone.

194
00:19:28,540 --> 00:19:38,320
 So it requires a very strong couple in order to successfully attain this tooth movement.

195
00:19:38,320 --> 00:19:44,400
 Now then we have rotation where the ratio is irrelevant it doesn't really exist because

196
00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:51,080
 this time we don't really have a moment of a force here the denominators equal effectively

197
00:19:51,080 --> 00:19:59,440
 to zero and we're making a first order couple to spin the tooth around its long axis.

198
00:19:59,440 --> 00:20:05,880
 So pure rotation refers to rotation of a tooth about its long axis.

199
00:20:05,880 --> 00:20:11,760
 So now we're looking down on the tooth from an occlusal view and again hypothetically we

200
00:20:11,760 --> 00:20:17,080
 don't have a moment of a force which would be the denominator in the ratio and a couple

201
00:20:17,080 --> 00:20:23,760
 alone is applied to a tooth causing it to rotate about a center of resistance.

202
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:29,560
 Here we're looking at this premolar again from an occlusal view and it's spinning around

203
00:20:29,560 --> 00:20:41,120
 to straighten it out in the arch and the center of rotation is at the center of resistance.

204
00:20:41,120 --> 00:20:47,960
 Now here's a helpful diagram listing the names of different types of directional tooth movement.

205
00:20:47,960 --> 00:20:53,520
 So protraction refers to forward movement, retraction, backward movement.

206
00:20:53,520 --> 00:21:01,080
 Those are your bodily movements of course intrusion and extrusion are also technically

207
00:21:01,080 --> 00:21:08,440
 bodily movement because it's another another version of pure translation.

208
00:21:08,440 --> 00:21:14,480
 Tipping the crown forward is proclination tipping it backward is retroclination and

209
00:21:14,480 --> 00:21:20,960
 then we have labial or buckle root torque and lingual or palatal root torque.

210
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:26,600
 So just a nice summary slide I put together to help explain the names of some of those

211
00:21:26,600 --> 00:21:29,640
 tooth movements.

212
00:21:29,640 --> 00:21:33,600
 And finally let's have a discussion about anchorage.

213
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:40,520
 So orthodontic anchorage is defined as resistance to unwanted tooth movement and it's based

214
00:21:40,520 --> 00:21:46,520
 on the idea of Newton's third law that for every action there is an equal and opposite

215
00:21:46,520 --> 00:21:47,520
 reaction.

216
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:54,560
 In other words for every desired tooth movement there is an equal and opposite potentially

217
00:21:54,560 --> 00:21:57,600
 undesirable tooth movement.

218
00:21:57,600 --> 00:22:03,360
 Now a really helpful way to think about this is teeth undergoing orthodontic treatment

219
00:22:03,360 --> 00:22:06,780
 are in a constant tug of war.

220
00:22:06,780 --> 00:22:14,420
 So if we're trying to pull these teeth with let's say there's a space here and we're closing

221
00:22:14,420 --> 00:22:20,160
 space with an elastomeric chain we're trying to pull these teeth this direction well these

222
00:22:20,160 --> 00:22:23,920
 teeth will naturally get pulled this way.

223
00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:30,400
 So the goal of treatment is to produce more intentional desired tooth movement as opposed

224
00:22:30,400 --> 00:22:35,280
 to unintentional undesirable tooth movement.

225
00:22:35,280 --> 00:22:40,920
 Now here's another reason why light force is often preferred whenever we can have it

226
00:22:40,920 --> 00:22:46,440
 because light force produces desired movement with little side effects.

227
00:22:46,440 --> 00:22:49,280
 So there's less anchorage toll.

228
00:22:49,280 --> 00:22:54,800
 In other words there's less equal and opposite force that we have to worry about.

229
00:22:54,800 --> 00:23:01,320
 On the contrary really heavy force produces our desired tooth movement sure but it has

230
00:23:01,320 --> 00:23:09,880
 more deleterious side effects and is more likely to put a toll on our anchorage.

231
00:23:09,880 --> 00:23:16,960
 So just like in tug of war the bigger and stronger participants will contribute more.

232
00:23:16,960 --> 00:23:25,080
 And so the bigger the surface area of the combined peridontal ligaments the more anchorage value

233
00:23:25,080 --> 00:23:31,760
 a tooth or unit of teeth will have together and the more they will be able to resist tooth

234
00:23:31,760 --> 00:23:32,760
 movement.

235
00:23:32,760 --> 00:23:38,880
 So in this example if again we were trying to close this premolar extraction space and

236
00:23:38,880 --> 00:23:46,520
 we were pitting these three anterior teeth against these three posterior teeth which unit

237
00:23:46,520 --> 00:23:52,040
 of teeth do you think is going to move more.

238
00:23:52,040 --> 00:23:56,200
 So if you said the anterior unit you were exactly right.

239
00:23:56,200 --> 00:24:04,040
 They're getting overpowered by the overwhelming amount of PDL surface area in the posterior

240
00:24:04,040 --> 00:24:08,760
 and so these back teeth are going to win the tug of war.

241
00:24:08,760 --> 00:24:14,920
 The same thing goes if we were trying to open the space with a coil spring for instance.

242
00:24:14,920 --> 00:24:20,520
 The front teeth would be pushed forward more than the back teeth would be pushed backward.

243
00:24:20,520 --> 00:24:28,640
 Again this unit of teeth has more surface area thanks to these multi rooted molars and they're

244
00:24:28,640 --> 00:24:34,720
 not going to budge as much as this anterior unit of teeth.

245
00:24:34,720 --> 00:24:40,040
 So one application of this is called reciprocal anchorage.

246
00:24:40,040 --> 00:24:45,840
 We now know that forces applied to one tooth unit cause an equal and opposite reactionary

247
00:24:45,840 --> 00:24:52,720
 force on another tooth unit and if both units have equal anchorage values that means that

248
00:24:52,720 --> 00:24:59,920
 the PDL area is the same then they will both experience equal and opposite tooth movement.

249
00:24:59,920 --> 00:25:09,160
 So not only is the force equal and opposite but so is the movement that those teeth experience.

250
00:25:09,160 --> 00:25:14,480
 So movement of one tooth can be pitted against another exactly like it.

251
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:18,600
 Like for diastemic closure between number eight and nine here.

252
00:25:18,600 --> 00:25:24,900
 They both have roughly similar peridontal ligament surface areas.

253
00:25:24,900 --> 00:25:29,960
 You can also pit a group of teeth against another group of teeth each with equal anchorage

254
00:25:29,960 --> 00:25:41,000
 values such as a central lateral and canine and a second molar and a first molar.

255
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:45,640
 So it would be like in this example except we leave out the second molar here.

256
00:25:45,640 --> 00:25:52,280
 These two units would be roughly equal to one another.

257
00:25:52,280 --> 00:25:58,800
 Now the way to tip the scales in one direction or the other is called reinforced anchorage

258
00:25:58,800 --> 00:26:03,400
 and there are several ways to reinforce our anchorage.

259
00:26:03,400 --> 00:26:08,680
 In our tug of war analogy I equate this to just adding more people or adding stronger

260
00:26:08,680 --> 00:26:11,960
 people to one of the two teams.

261
00:26:11,960 --> 00:26:17,840
 So we can do this by adding more teeth to the anchor unit and that would distribute that

262
00:26:17,840 --> 00:26:23,880
 equal and opposite reaction force over a larger PDL area.

263
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:27,920
 So the anchor unit doesn't move as much.

264
00:26:27,920 --> 00:26:33,640
 Now we can also add extra oral sources like the back of the head for instance.

265
00:26:33,640 --> 00:26:40,240
 So head gear can be used to augment anchorage as well but poor patient compliance they don't

266
00:26:40,240 --> 00:26:46,120
 like to wear it and heavy intermittent forces from the head gear if it's being taken on

267
00:26:46,120 --> 00:26:52,820
 and off all throughout the day are not good ways to counterbalance the light continuous

268
00:26:52,820 --> 00:26:57,040
 force from orthodontic appliances.

269
00:26:57,040 --> 00:27:04,000
 So those are just two examples of how we can reinforce our anchorage unit.

270
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:08,280
 Another way to really tip the scales is skeletal anchorage.

271
00:27:08,280 --> 00:27:14,400
 So in our tug of war analogy I equate this to one side basically cheating and placing

272
00:27:14,400 --> 00:27:19,440
 metal stakes in the ground and then planting their feet behind those stakes.

273
00:27:19,440 --> 00:27:26,160
 Now the stakes can fall out but if not that team is not going to move at all.

274
00:27:26,160 --> 00:27:33,320
 So our metal stakes here are called temporary anchorage devices and tads act like ankle

275
00:27:33,320 --> 00:27:36,040
 lifts teeth when they are successful.

276
00:27:36,040 --> 00:27:41,880
 They are not going to budge and so they are screwed into bone and they are not intended

277
00:27:41,880 --> 00:27:45,780
 to move unless they fail and fall out.

278
00:27:45,780 --> 00:27:52,560
 So bone screws are placed in the alveolar bone and they provide anchors for moving usually

279
00:27:52,560 --> 00:28:00,800
 specific teeth whereas bone plates are attached to basal bone beneath the teeth by multiple

280
00:28:00,800 --> 00:28:06,000
 bone screws and they provide an anchor for more extensive tooth movement.

281
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:11,440
 The bone screws are generally less invasive but are potentially less stable whereas bone

282
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:18,860
 plates are more invasive but can be more stable because multiple screws are being utilized.

283
00:28:18,860 --> 00:28:26,160
 The lower limit for tad placement is generally around age 11 when the bone is mature enough

284
00:28:26,160 --> 00:28:34,080
 in order to hold these tads with some amount of stability.

285
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:42,280
 For these many implants this concept of osteo integration that we talked about with permanent

286
00:28:42,280 --> 00:28:49,600
 implants is not necessary and perhaps not desirable because they are meant to be temporary and

287
00:28:49,600 --> 00:28:57,000
 we don't want them being permanently fixed biologically to the bone.

288
00:28:57,000 --> 00:29:04,000
 Tads are particularly useful for distalizing and/or intruding molars which can be a notoriously

289
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:09,080
 difficult thing to do without them.

290
00:29:09,080 --> 00:29:15,560
 So let's talk through some examples of anchorage in premolar extraction cases.

291
00:29:15,560 --> 00:29:22,760
 So for a maximum anchorage case this might involve a severe crowding and/or incisor protrusion

292
00:29:22,760 --> 00:29:29,720
 and those incisors are out too far and we want to favor the front teeth moving back and

293
00:29:29,720 --> 00:29:35,800
 keep the anchor teeth where they are and we don't want those to move very much at all.

294
00:29:35,800 --> 00:29:42,080
 So not more than a quarter of this extraction space should be closed by the anchor teeth

295
00:29:42,080 --> 00:29:44,520
 moving forward.

296
00:29:44,520 --> 00:29:49,920
 In a moderate anchorage case the anchor teeth can be permitted to move forward about a quarter

297
00:29:49,920 --> 00:29:54,880
 or a half way into this extraction space and the same can be said for the front teeth moving

298
00:29:54,880 --> 00:29:55,880
 back.

299
00:29:55,880 --> 00:30:01,040
 More or less this is some reciprocal space closure.

300
00:30:01,040 --> 00:30:06,000
 And for a minimum anchorage case the anchorage demand is very low and the anchor teeth are

301
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:12,400
 permitted to move forward into that extraction space and we often want them to do so while

302
00:30:12,400 --> 00:30:16,800
 the front teeth this time we would prefer not to move back.

303
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:24,040
 This often requires some assistance with skeletal anchors or taking out second premolars instead

304
00:30:24,040 --> 00:30:30,600
 of first premolars because then you're pitting more front teeth you're reinforcing that front

305
00:30:30,600 --> 00:30:35,520
 anchorage against a smaller amount of back teeth.

306
00:30:35,520 --> 00:30:41,800
 So those are just some examples of how we can use anchorage in order to treatment plan

307
00:30:41,800 --> 00:30:44,320
 our cases.

308
00:30:44,320 --> 00:30:48,520
 All right and that's it for this video guys thank you so much for watching I know it was

309
00:30:48,520 --> 00:30:53,960
 a lot of technical jargon and terms but I really hope I was able to simplify it for

310
